\begin_body
\begin_layout Title
The centered Magnus exponent and Magnus quadrature formulas of orders 2, 4, 6 and 8
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Kenneth C. Johnson
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

ken@kjinnovation.com
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
Version 09-Nov-2026
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "https://ai.vixra.org/abs/2611.0005"
target "https://ai.vixra.org/abs/2611.0005"
literal "false"
\end_inset
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Abstract
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
The Magnus exponent is formulated over an integration interval 
\begin_inset Formula $[-t,t]$
\end_inset
 that is centered on the origin, so that the solution at 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset
 is obtained from the solution at 
\begin_inset Formula $-t$
\end_inset
. In this centered formulation the propagator satisfies 
\begin_inset Formula $U(-t)=U(t)^{-1}$
\end_inset
, the Magnus exponent is an odd function of 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset
, and the Magnus theorem takes a form in which the even and odd parts of the coefficient matrix appear separately. The centered formulation, the Legendre-moment representation and an order-6 formula are derived in an introductory tutorial 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "jtut"
literal "false"
\end_inset
. Here the Magnus exponent is expressed in Legendre moments of the coefficient matrix over the centered interval, and Legendre-moment approximations with residual errors of order 
\begin_inset Formula $t^3$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $t^5$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $t^7$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $t^9$
\end_inset
 are given, using 0, 1, 3 and 6 commutators, with the moments evaluated by Gauss-Legendre, Newton-Cotes or Gauss-Lobatto quadrature, and Gauss-Kronrod rules are shown to give embedded error estimates. The methodology and results are compared with those of Blanes et al. 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bcr2000"
literal "false"
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bcr2002"
literal "false"
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bcor2009"
literal "false"
\end_inset
. A companion paper 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "j10"
literal "false"
\end_inset
 extends the formulas to order 10.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The introductory tutorial 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "jtut"
literal "false"
\end_inset
 derives the centered formulation of the Magnus exponent, the commutator and adjoint-operator relations it uses, the centered Magnus theorem, the Legendre polynomials and centered Legendre moments, and an order-6 Magnus quadrature formula with three commutators. That material is not repeated here. This section summarizes the results that are used below, and the rest of the paper extends the tutorial to the Magnus exponent series in Legendre moments, Legendre-moment expansions of orders 2 to 8, alternative quadrature rules for the moments, test results, and a comparison with the work of Blanes et al.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The linear differential equation 
\begin_inset Formula $F'(t)=A(t)\cdot F(t)$
\end_inset
, where 
\begin_inset Formula $A(t)$
\end_inset
 is a square-matrix function of scalar argument 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset
, is solved over the centered interval 
\begin_inset Formula $[-t,t]$
\end_inset
: the value 
\begin_inset Formula $F(-t)$
\end_inset
 is specified and 
\begin_inset Formula $F(t)$
\end_inset
 is to be determined. (A nonhomogeneous equation can be put in this form 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "jtut"
literal "false"
\end_inset
, and any interval can be centered by shifting the time origin to its midpoint; an interval of length 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset
 centered at 
\begin_inset Formula $t_0$
\end_inset
 corresponds to 
\begin_inset Formula $t=h/2$
\end_inset
, with 
\begin_inset Formula $A(\tau)$
\end_inset
 replaced by 
\begin_inset Formula $A(t_0+\tau)$
\end_inset
.) The solution is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
F(t)=U(t)\cdot F(-t)\label{eq:centered}
\end{equation}

\end_inset
where the centered propagator 
\begin_inset Formula $U(t)$
\end_inset
 is defined by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
U'(t)=A(t)\cdot U(t)+U(t)\cdot A(-t),\quad U(0)=\mathbf{I}\label{eq:uode}
\end{equation}

\end_inset
and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{I}$
\end_inset
 is the identity matrix. 
\begin_inset Formula $U(t)$
\end_inset
 is related to the conventional propagator 
\begin_inset Formula $V(t)$
\end_inset
, defined by 
\begin_inset Formula $V'(t)=A(t)\cdot V(t)$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $V(0)=\mathbf{I}$
\end_inset
, by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
U(t)=V(t)\cdot V(-t)^{-1}\label{eq:uv}
\end{equation}

\end_inset
(because 
\begin_inset Formula $F(t)=V(t)\cdot F(0)$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $F(-t)=V(-t)\cdot F(0)$
\end_inset
), and replacing 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset
 by 
\begin_inset Formula $-t$
\end_inset
 gives the time-reversal property
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
U(-t)=U(t)^{-1}\label{eq:uinv}
\end{equation}

\end_inset
The centered propagator and Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:uinv"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
 also appear in Blanes et al. 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bcor2009"
literal "false"
\end_inset
 (its Eqs. 63-66), where they are used to show that the Magnus exponent of a step contains only odd powers of the step size.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 
\emph on
centered Magnus exponent
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega(t)$
\end_inset
 is defined by 
\begin_inset Formula $U(t)=\exp(\Omega(t))$
\end_inset
 with 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega(0)=\mathbf{0}$
\end_inset
. For 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset
 small enough that the matrix logarithm near 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{I}$
\end_inset
 is unique, Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:uinv"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
 implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset
 is an odd function of 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset
,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\Omega(-t)=-\Omega(t)\label{eq:odd}
\end{equation}

\end_inset
With the commutator 
\begin_inset Formula $[X,Y]=X\cdot Y-Y\cdot X$
\end_inset
 and the adjoint mapping 
\begin_inset Formula $ad_{\Omega}^{0}(X)=X$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $ad_{\Omega}^{j}(X)=[\Omega,ad_{\Omega}^{j-1}(X)]$
\end_inset
 (
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset
 nested commutators with 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset
), 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega(t)$
\end_inset
 satisfies the 
\emph on
centered Magnus theorem
\emph default
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "jtut"
literal "false"
\end_inset
,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\Omega'(t)&=\sum_{j}c_{j}\cdot ad_{\Omega(t)}^{2j}\big(A(t)+A(-t)\big)-\tfrac{1}{2}\cdot\big[\Omega(t),A(t)-A(-t)\big]\\
&=A(t)+A(-t)-\tfrac{1}{2}\cdot[\Omega(t),A(t)-A(-t)]+\tfrac{1}{12}\cdot[\Omega(t),[\Omega(t),A(t)+A(-t)]]\\
&\quad-\tfrac{1}{720}\cdot[\Omega(t),[\Omega(t),[\Omega(t),[\Omega(t),A(t)+A(-t)]]]]+O(t^{6})
\end{aligned}\label{eq:evenodd}
\end{equation}

\end_inset
where 
\begin_inset Formula $c_j=B_{2j}/(2j)!$
\end_inset
 (
\begin_inset Formula $c_0=1$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $c_1=\frac{1}{12}$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $c_2=-\frac{1}{720}$
\end_inset
, …) and the 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{2j}$
\end_inset
 are Bernoulli numbers. The even part 
\begin_inset Formula $A(t)+A(-t)$
\end_inset
 and the odd part 
\begin_inset Formula $A(t)-A(-t)$
\end_inset
 of the coefficient matrix appear separately. If 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega(t)$
\end_inset
 is odd, each term on the right side of Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:evenodd"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
 is even (
\begin_inset Formula $ad_{\Omega(t)}^{2j}$
\end_inset
 preserves the parity of 
\begin_inset Formula $A(t)+A(-t)$
\end_inset
, and one commutator with the odd 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega(t)$
\end_inset
 turns the odd 
\begin_inset Formula $A(t)-A(-t)$
\end_inset
 into an even function), so its integral with 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega(0)=\mathbf{0}$
\end_inset
 is odd, consistent with Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:odd"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
. When 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset
 is expanded in a Taylor series about 
\begin_inset Formula $t=0$
\end_inset
 and Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:evenodd"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
 is solved by successive approximation, every term of the series for 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega(t)$
\end_inset
 is therefore an odd power of 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset
; the leading term is 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega(t)=\int_{-t}^{t}A(\tau)\,d\tau+O(t^3)$
\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Centered Legendre moments
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The formulas below express 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega(t)$
\end_inset
 in terms of the Legendre moments of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset
 over the centered interval 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "jtut"
literal "false"
\end_inset
,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
g_{k}(t)=\int_{-t}^{t}P_{k}(\tau/t)\cdot A(\tau)\,d\tau\label{eq:gdef}
\end{equation}

\end_inset
where 
\begin_inset Formula $P_k$
\end_inset
 is the Legendre polynomial of degree 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset
 (see 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "jtut"
literal "false"
\end_inset
 for the definition and properties of the Legendre polynomials). 
\begin_inset Formula $g_0(t)$
\end_inset
 is the integral of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset
 over the interval, and if 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset
 is constant then 
\begin_inset Formula $g_k(t)=\mathbf{0}$
\end_inset
 for 
\begin_inset Formula $k\ge1$
\end_inset
. As shown in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "jtut"
literal "false"
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $g_k(t)=O(t^{k+1})$
\end_inset
; more precisely, with 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset
 expanded in a Taylor series about 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau=0$
\end_inset
 the leading term of 
\begin_inset Formula $g_k(t)$
\end_inset
 is 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{kk}\cdot t^{k+1}\cdot A^{(k)}(0)/k!$
\end_inset
, with 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{kk}=\int_{-1}^{1}P_k(u)\cdot u^k\,du=2^{k+1}\cdot(k!)^2/(2k+1)!$
\end_inset
. We call 
\begin_inset Formula $k+1$
\end_inset
 the 
\emph on
weight
\emph default
 (aka order) of 
\begin_inset Formula $g_k$
\end_inset
; a term of weight 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset
 is 
\begin_inset Formula $O(t^n)$
\end_inset
 and the weight of a product is the sum of its factors' weights. Replacing 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset
 by 
\begin_inset Formula $-t$
\end_inset
 in Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:gdef"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
, substituting 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau\to-\tau$
\end_inset
 and using the parity 
\begin_inset Formula $P_k(-u)=(-1)^k\cdot P_k(u)$
\end_inset
 shows that the moments have definite parity,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
g_{k}(-t)=(-1)^{k+1}\cdot g_{k}(t)\label{eq:gpar}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

\begin_inset Formula $g_0$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $g_2$
\end_inset
 are odd functions of 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $g_1$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $g_3$
\end_inset
 are even, and a product of moments of weight 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset
 has parity 
\begin_inset Formula $(-1)^n$
\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
The Magnus exponent in Legendre moments
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The tutorial 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "jtut"
literal "false"
\end_inset
 expresses 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega(t)$
\end_inset
 in the moments through order 
\begin_inset Formula $t^7$
\end_inset
 by Picard iteration of Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:evenodd"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
, with 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset
 expanded in Legendre polynomials. Here the Taylor-series solution of Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:evenodd"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
 was re-expressed in the moments by inverting the Taylor expansions of the 
\begin_inset Formula $g_k$
\end_inset
, in exact rational arithmetic, by successive approximation through order 
\begin_inset Formula $t^9$
\end_inset
. Through weight 7 the result is, in the Lyndon basis of nested commutators,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\Omega(t)={}&g_{0}\\
&-\tfrac{1}{2}[g_{0},g_{1}]\\
&-\tfrac{1}{2}[g_{1},g_{2}]+\tfrac{1}{12}[g_{0},[g_{0},g_{2}]]\\
&+\tfrac{3}{20}[[g_{0},g_{1}],g_{1}]+\tfrac{1}{120}[g_{0},[g_{0},[g_{0},g_{1}]]]\\
&-\tfrac{1}{2}[g_{2},g_{3}]+\tfrac{1}{10}[g_{0},[g_{1},g_{3}]]\\
&+\tfrac{5}{84}[[g_{0},g_{2}],g_{2}]-\tfrac{1}{20}[[g_{0},g_{3}],g_{1}]\\
&-\tfrac{3}{14}[g_{1},[g_{1},g_{2}]]-\tfrac{1}{120}[g_{0},[g_{0},[g_{0},g_{3}]]]\\
&-\tfrac{1}{168}[g_{0},[g_{0},[g_{1},g_{2}]]]-\tfrac{1}{28}[g_{0},[[g_{0},g_{2}],g_{1}]]\\
&-\tfrac{3}{56}[[g_{0},g_{1}],[g_{0},g_{2}]]-\tfrac{9}{280}[[[g_{0},g_{1}],g_{1}],g_{1}]\\
&-\tfrac{1}{504}[g_{0},[g_{0},[g_{0},[g_{0},g_{2}]]]]-\tfrac{1}{280}[g_{0},[g_{0},[[g_{0},g_{1}],g_{1}]]]\\
&+\tfrac{1}{210}[[g_{0},[g_{0},g_{1}]],[g_{0},g_{1}]]-\tfrac{1}{5040}[g_{0},[g_{0},[g_{0},[g_{0},[g_{0},g_{1}]]]]]\\
&+O(t^{9})
\end{aligned}\label{eq:target}
\end{equation}

\end_inset
Three properties of this series are used below. (i) Only odd weights occur, as Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:odd"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
 requires. (ii) The only term outside commutators is 
\begin_inset Formula $g_0$
\end_inset
. (iii) Through weight 
\begin_inset Formula $2n-1$
\end_inset
, only 
\begin_inset Formula $g_0,\ldots,g_{n-1}$
\end_inset
 occur; 
\begin_inset Formula $g_k$
\end_inset
 first appears at weight 
\begin_inset Formula $2k+1$
\end_inset
. A formula of order 
\begin_inset Formula $2n$
\end_inset
, with residual 
\begin_inset Formula $O(t^{2n+1})$
\end_inset
, therefore needs only the moments 
\begin_inset Formula $g_0,\ldots,g_{n-1}$
\end_inset
, and it must reproduce the coefficients of the series at odd weights up to 
\begin_inset Formula $2n-1$
\end_inset
 (the 
\emph on
order conditions
\emph default
, Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:conditions"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
):
\end_layout

