 nodes give a rule exact for polynomials of degree 
\begin_inset Formula $3n+1$
\end_inset
 (
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset
 even) or 
\begin_inset Formula $3n+2$
\end_inset
 (
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset
 odd). One set of samples then gives the moments of an order-
\begin_inset Formula $2n$
\end_inset
 formula from the Gauss nodes alone and those of a higher-order formula from all 
\begin_inset Formula $2n+1$
\end_inset
 nodes; the difference of the two results estimates the error of the lower-order result and is a conservative bound for that of the higher-order one. For 
\begin_inset Formula $n=1$
\end_inset
 the Kronrod extension of the midpoint rule is the three-node Gauss-Legendre rule (orders 2 and 6). For 
\begin_inset Formula $n=2$
\end_inset
 the five nodes 
\begin_inset Formula $0$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $\pm1/\sqrt{3}$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\pm\sqrt{6/7}$
\end_inset
, with weights 
\begin_inset Formula $14/45$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $27/110$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $49/495$
\end_inset
 respectively, give a rule of degree 7, so five samples give order-4 and order-8 results with 
\begin_inset Formula $1+6=7$
\end_inset
 commutators. For 
\begin_inset Formula $n=3$
\end_inset
 the seven-node rule (degree 11) serves order 8, and the companion paper 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "j10"
literal "false"
\end_inset
 gives an embedded order-6/order-10 pair on it that reuses the three order-6 commutators. The Kronrod nodes do not include the endpoints, so all 
\begin_inset Formula $2n+1$
\end_inset
 samples are new in each step.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Legendre-moment expansions of the Magnus exponent
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The following formulas are approximations to the Magnus exponent derived from Eq. 49 of the tutorial 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "jtut"
literal "false"
\end_inset
, the integral form of the centered Magnus theorem (Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:evenodd"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
). Successive approximation of that equation gives the series of Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:target"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
, and the formula of order 
\begin_inset Formula $2n$
\end_inset
 reproduces the series through weight 
\begin_inset Formula $2n-1$
\end_inset
, with the fewest commutators found (Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:conditions"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
). The formulas apply to exact moments or to moments computed with any rule of the preceding section of polynomial degree at least 
\begin_inset Formula $2n-1$
\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\emph on
Order 2
\emph default
 (no commutators):
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\Omega(t)=g_{0}(t)+O(t^{3})\label{eq:f2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset
With one Gauss-Legendre node this is the midpoint rule, 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega(t)=2t\cdot A(0)+O(t^3)$
\end_inset
; with two Newton-Cotes nodes it is the trapezoid rule, 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega(t)=t\cdot(A(-t)+A(t))+O(t^3)$
\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\emph on
Order 4
\emph default
 (one commutator):
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\Omega(t)=g_{0}-\tfrac{1}{2}\cdot[g_{0},g_{1}]+O(t^{5})\label{eq:f4}
\end{equation}

\end_inset
In terms of samples, with Gauss-Legendre nodes (
\begin_inset Formula $a_\pm=A(\pm t/\sqrt{3})$
\end_inset
) and with Newton-Cotes nodes, respectively,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\Omega(t)&=t\cdot(a_{-}+a_{+})-\tfrac{t^{2}}{\sqrt{3}}\cdot[a_{-},a_{+}]+O(t^{5})\\
\Omega(t)&=\tfrac{t}{3}\cdot(A(-t)+4\cdot A(0)+A(t))-\tfrac{t^{2}}{3}\cdot[A(0),A(t)-A(-t)]+O(t^{5})
\end{aligned}\label{eq:f4s}
\end{equation}

\end_inset
(The second form differs from Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:f4"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
 with the moments of Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:nc"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
 by terms of order 
\begin_inset Formula $t^5$
\end_inset
.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\emph on
Order 6
\emph default
 (three commutators):
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
C_{1}&=\big[g_{0},\ g_{1}\big]\\
C_{2}&=\big[g_{0},\ g_{2}+\tfrac{1}{10}C_{1}\big]\\
\Omega(t)&=g_{0}-\tfrac{1}{2}\big[g_{0}-g_{2}-\tfrac{3}{10}C_{1},\ g_{1}-\tfrac{1}{6}C_{2}\big]+O(t^{7})
\end{aligned}\label{eq:f6}
\end{equation}

\end_inset
This is the formula derived step by step in the tutorial 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "jtut"
literal "false"
\end_inset
, where 
\begin_inset Formula $C_1$
\end_inset
 is denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\emph on
Order 8
\emph default
 (six commutators):
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
C_{1}&=\big[-\tfrac{1}{2}g_{0}+\tfrac{5}{7}g_{2},\ g_{1}-g_{3}\big]\\
C_{2}&=\big[g_{0}-\tfrac{5}{2}g_{2},\ 5g_{2}-C_{1}\big]\\
C_{3}&=\big[\tfrac{1}{2}g_{0}-\tfrac{5}{7}g_{2}+\tfrac{3}{14}C_{1},\ -2g_{1}+2g_{3}+\tfrac{1}{21}C_{2}\big]\\
C_{4}&=\big[\tfrac{3}{14}C_{1},\ -g_{1}+\tfrac{7}{2}g_{3}\big]\\
C_{5}&=\big[2g_{0}-5g_{2}+\tfrac{15}{14}C_{1},\ 10g_{2}-C_{3}+C_{4}\big]\\
C_{6}&=\big[g_{0}-C_{1}+C_{3},\ 3g_{1}+7g_{3}+\tfrac{1}{2}C_{2}-\tfrac{1}{6}C_{5}\big]\\
\Omega(t)&=g_{0}+\tfrac{7}{20}C_{3}-\tfrac{1}{20}C_{6}+O(t^{9})
\end{aligned}\label{eq:f8}
\end{equation}

\end_inset
Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:f8"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
 is the scheme of Blanes, Casas and Ros 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bcr2002"
literal "false"
\end_inset
 rewritten in Legendre moments, with the commutator operands rescaled to simplify the coefficients. All coefficients in Eqs. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:f2"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
-
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:f8"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
 are exact rationals. Each formula was verified in exact rational arithmetic, by expanding it in the free algebra generated by the moments and comparing every term through weight 
\begin_inset Formula $2n$
\end_inset
 with Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:target"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
, both with exact moments and with the moments of all the quadrature rules above (those with irrational nodes in floating-point or high-precision arithmetic).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Integration of the differential equation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
One integration step of length 
\begin_inset Formula $h=2t$
\end_inset
, from 
\begin_inset Formula $t_c-t$
\end_inset
 to 
\begin_inset Formula $t_c+t$
\end_inset
, proceeds as follows (with 
\begin_inset Formula $A(\tau)$
\end_inset
 replaced by 
\begin_inset Formula $A(t_c+\tau)$
\end_inset
 in the preceding sections):
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Sample 
\begin_inset Formula $A(t_c+u_i\cdot t)$
\end_inset
 at the nodes of the chosen rule: the 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset
 Gauss-Legendre nodes, the 
\begin_inset Formula $2n+1$
\end_inset
 Gauss-Kronrod nodes, or the Newton-Cotes or Gauss-Lobatto nodes, whose first sample is the last sample of the previous step.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Form the moments 
\begin_inset Formula $g_0,\ldots,g_{n-1}$
\end_inset
 from Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:gauss"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
, with the nodes and weights of the chosen Gauss-type rule, or from Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:nc"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Evaluate the commutators and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega(t)$
\end_inset
 from the formula of the chosen order (Eqs. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:f2"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
-
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:f8"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Advance the solution, 
\begin_inset Formula $F(t_c+t)=\exp(\Omega(t))\cdot F(t_c-t)$
\end_inset
, with the matrix exponential evaluated by a standard method (for example, scaling and squaring), or with its action on 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset
 evaluated directly.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
The cost per step (Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:cost"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
) is one matrix exponential or its action, the commutators (two matrix products each), and the samples of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset
. When 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset
 is skew-Hermitian (as for the Schrödinger equation) or real skew-symmetric, the moments and all commutators are too, so every operand is skew-Hermitian and 
\begin_inset Formula $Y\cdot X=(X\cdot Y)^{\dagger}$
\end_inset
; each commutator then needs only one matrix product. The same holds for Hamiltonian 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset
, with 
\begin_inset Formula $Y\cdot X=-J\cdot(X\cdot Y)^{T}\cdot J$
\end_inset
 for the symplectic matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $J$
\end_inset
. (It does not hold for Hermitian or symmetric 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset
, because their commutators are not Hermitian or symmetric.)
\end_layout

