\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
Halving 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset
 reduces the error by factors approaching 
\begin_inset Formula $2^{2n+1}=8$
\end_inset
, 32, 128 and 512, confirming the residual orders. In the first two tests all the quadrature rules give errors within about 10% of those with exact moments. In the rapidly varying test the quadrature error of 
\begin_inset Formula $g_0$
\end_inset
 dominates; there the Gauss-Legendre, Newton-Cotes and Gauss-Lobatto rules remain within a factor of about 1.4 of each other at orders 4-8 (2.3 at order 2), the five-node Gauss-Kronrod rule is about 2.6 times more accurate than the four-node Gauss-Legendre rule at order 8, and exact moments would be 3-6 times more accurate. In all three tests the Gauss-Kronrod error estimate for order 4 (the difference between the order-8 result with five Kronrod nodes and the order-4 result with the two Gauss nodes) agrees with the actual order-4 error to within about 3%.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Comparison with Blanes et al.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Blanes, Casas and Ros 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bcr2000"
literal "false"
\end_inset
 introduced Magnus integrators built from univariate moments of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset
 (the integrals 
\begin_inset Formula $B^{(i)}$
\end_inset
 below) with 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset
 expanded about the midpoint of the step, giving schemes of orders 4, 6 and 8 with 1, 4 and 10 commutators, evaluated with either Gauss-Legendre or Newton-Cotes quadrature. In 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bcr2002"
literal "false"
\end_inset
 they reduced the commutator counts to 1, 3 and 6, by optimizing in a graded free Lie algebra, and stated that these are the minimum numbers; for order 8 they showed that their five-commutator ansatz has no real solution. Their order-8 scheme is the only published one of that order known to us. The review by Blanes, Casas, Oteo and Ros 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bcor2009"
literal "false"
\end_inset
 presents the same framework, with explicit order-4 and order-6 schemes for both Gauss-Legendre and Newton-Cotes nodes (its Eqs. 251-258), and cites the six-commutator order-8 scheme. The present formulation differs from theirs in the following respects.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\emph on
Formulation.
\emph default
 Ref. 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bcor2009"
literal "false"
\end_inset
 writes the solution over a step in the centered form 
\begin_inset Formula $Y(t_{1/2}+s/2)=\exp(\Omega(t_{1/2}+s/2,t_{1/2}-s/2))\cdot Y(t_{1/2}-s/2)$
\end_inset
 (its Eq. 63), which is Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:centered"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
 with 
\begin_inset Formula $s=2t$
\end_inset
, and uses it to establish the time symmetry; the integrators are then derived from the conventional Magnus equation over 
\begin_inset Formula $[t_n,t_n+h]$
\end_inset
 with 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset
 expanded about the midpoint. Here the centered relation is the starting point: the propagator equation (Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:uode"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
) and the centered Magnus theorem (Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:evenodd"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
) are written directly for the centered interval, so the time symmetry (
\begin_inset Formula $U(-t)=U(t)^{-1}$
\end_inset
, Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:uinv"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
; 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega(t)$
\end_inset
 odd, Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:odd"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
) is built into the differential equation, which separates the even and odd parts of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset
. We have not found the centered differential equation in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bcr2000"
literal "false"
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bcr2002"
literal "false"
\end_inset
 or 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bcor2009"
literal "false"
\end_inset
. The resulting series is the same as theirs.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\emph on
Moments.
\emph default
 Ref. 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bcr2002"
literal "false"
\end_inset
 uses the moments 
\begin_inset Formula $B^{(i)}=h^{-i}\int(\tau-t_{n}-h/2)^{i}\cdot A(\tau)\,d\tau$
\end_inset
 over the step, which in the centered notation are 
\begin_inset Formula $B^{(i)}=(2t)^{-i}\int_{-t}^{t}\tau^{i}\cdot A(\tau)\,d\tau$
\end_inset
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bcr2000"
literal "false"
\end_inset
 divides by an extra factor 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset
, and 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bcor2009"
literal "false"
\end_inset
 calls them 
\begin_inset Formula $A^{(i)}$
\end_inset
). In Legendre moments,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
B^{(0)}=g_{0},\quad B^{(1)}=\tfrac{1}{2}\cdot g_{1},\quad B^{(2)}=\tfrac{1}{6}\cdot(g_{2}+\tfrac{1}{2}\cdot g_{0}),\quad B^{(3)}=\tfrac{1}{20}\cdot(g_{3}+\tfrac{3}{2}\cdot g_{1})\label{eq:bmom}
\end{equation}

\end_inset
The schemes of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bcr2002"
literal "false"
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bcor2009"
literal "false"
\end_inset
 are derived in the free Lie algebra generated by the midpoint Taylor terms 
\begin_inset Formula $b_i=h^{i}A^{(i-1)}(t_{1/2})/(i-1)!$
\end_inset
 (denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_i$
\end_inset
 in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bcor2009"
literal "false"
\end_inset
), and the 
\begin_inset Formula $b_i$
\end_inset
 are then replaced by linear combinations of the 
\begin_inset Formula $B^{(i)}$
\end_inset
 that agree with them to the order considered. For order 6 these combinations are 
\begin_inset Formula $g_0-\frac{5}{2}g_2$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $6g_1$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $30g_2$
\end_inset
, which are close to Legendre moments. Here the formulas are derived and verified directly in the Legendre moments, in which they hold exactly with no truncated change of variables. The orthogonality of the 
\begin_inset Formula $P_k$
\end_inset
 gives Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:target"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
 its simple structure (only 
\begin_inset Formula $g_0$
\end_inset
 outside commutators, and only 
\begin_inset Formula $g_0,\ldots,g_{n-1}$
\end_inset
 through order 
\begin_inset Formula $2n$
\end_inset
) and gives the simple accuracy criterion for the quadrature in the moment-quadrature section.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\emph on
Formulas.
\emph default
 At order 4 the formulas coincide with those of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bcr2000"
literal "false"
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bcr2002"
literal "false"
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bcor2009"
literal "false"
\end_inset
. At order 6, the three-commutator scheme of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bcr2002"
literal "false"
\end_inset
 (its Eqs. 3.5-3.7, repeated in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bcor2009"
literal "false"
\end_inset
 as its Eq. 251), translated to centered Legendre moments with Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:bmom"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
 (
\begin_inset Formula $b_1=g_0-\frac{5}{2}g_2$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $b_2=6g_1$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $b_3=30g_2$
\end_inset
) and with its commutators rescaled, is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
C_{1}&=\big[g_{0}-\tfrac{5}{2}g_{2},\ g_{1}\big]\\
C_{2}&=\big[g_{0}-\tfrac{5}{2}g_{2},\ g_{2}+\tfrac{1}{10}C_{1}\big]\\
\Omega(t)&=g_{0}-\tfrac{1}{2}\big[g_{0}-g_{2}-\tfrac{3}{10}C_{1},\ g_{1}-\tfrac{1}{6}C_{2}\big]+O(t^{7})
\end{aligned}\label{eq:bcr6}
\end{equation}

\end_inset
This is identical to Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:f6"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
 except for the substitution of 
\begin_inset Formula $g_0-\frac{5}{2}g_2$
\end_inset
 for 
\begin_inset Formula $g_0$
\end_inset
 in 
\begin_inset Formula $C_1$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $C_2$
\end_inset
, which changes 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset
 only at weight 7, the weight of the residual. The leading-error coefficients of Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:f6"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
 (the weight-7 residual, with each moment scaled by its leading size) have an 11% smaller norm; on the test problems the two agree to within about 10%. At order 8, Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:f8"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
 is the scheme of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bcr2002"
literal "false"
\end_inset
 (its Eqs. 3.8-3.10). A search over all six-commutator patterns, which goes beyond the single ansatz of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bcr2002"
literal "false"
\end_inset
, found six families of solutions with 5 to 12 free parameters each. Minimizing the leading error over the families reduced that norm by a factor of about 1.8 below the scheme of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bcr2002"
literal "false"
\end_inset
, but gave no improvement on the test problems, and the optimized coefficients are not simple numbers; the published order-8 scheme is therefore effectively optimal. (The optimization in the title of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bcr2002"
literal "false"
\end_inset
 refers to the number of commutators, not to the leading error.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\emph on
Quadrature.
\emph default
 As in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bcr2000"
literal "false"
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bcor2009"
literal "false"
\end_inset
, the moments can be computed with either Gauss-Legendre or Newton-Cotes nodes, and the Newton-Cotes endpoint sample can be reused in the next step. Ref. 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bcr2000"
literal "false"
\end_inset
 gives Newton-Cotes moment formulas for orders 6 and 8 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bcor2009"
literal "false"
\end_inset
 for orders 4 and 6, obtained by applying the Newton-Cotes rule to the products of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset
 with powers of the time variable; Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:nc"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
 gives interpolatory weights for all four orders, and the moment-quadrature section gives the general accuracy criterion (any rule of polynomial degree 
\begin_inset Formula $2n-1$
\end_inset
 suffices for order 
\begin_inset Formula $2n$
\end_inset
). The Gauss-Lobatto and Gauss-Kronrod rules given here are not used in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bcr2000"
literal "false"
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bcr2002"
literal "false"
\end_inset
 or 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bcor2009"
literal "false"
\end_inset
. The results agree with the observation in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bcr2002"
literal "false"
\end_inset
 that exact moments can be considerably more accurate than quadrature moments for some problems: in the rapidly varying test, exact moments are 3-6 times more accurate.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\emph on
Verification.
\emph default
 Every formula here was checked in exact rational arithmetic, and the Legendre-moment series itself was derived from the centered Magnus theorem and cross-checked against the conventional midpoint expansion.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Code availability
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Documentation, code and research resources are available in the GitHub repository 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "https://github.com/kjinnovation/explde"
target "https://github.com/kjinnovation/explde"
literal "false"
\end_inset
, and the code also on MATLAB File Exchange, 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "https://www.mathworks.com/matlabcentral/fileexchange/183695-explde"
target "https://www.mathworks.com/matlabcentral/fileexchange/183695-explde"
literal "false"
\end_inset
. The MATLAB function explde.m solves 
\begin_inset Formula $F'(t)=A(t)\cdot F(t)$
\end_inset
 with the formulas of orders 2 to 10 of this paper and 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "j10"
literal "false"
\end_inset
, using Gauss-Legendre moments (computed in magnus_exp.m); magnus10coef.m lists the order-10 coefficients. The repository also contains the tutorial 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "jtut"
literal "false"
\end_inset
, this paper and 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "j10"
literal "false"
\end_inset
 with their Word, LyX and LaTeX sources, and research resources: a guide to the derivation and verification methods, Python reference code in exact rational arithmetic, and the defining equations of the order-10 coefficients.
\end_layout

